{
 "_about": "STARTER TABLE, seeded 2026-08-28 from tools/census-citations.py over the 19 chapters then transcribed. Every alias below was OBSERVED in the corpus; the mappings are the transcriber's and are NOT verified against the plates. Check before the index ships — a wrong book in an index is worse than a missing one. Corrected 2026-08-28: `Jud.` was first mapped to Jude and is JUDGES — La `Jud. xiii. 18.` sits against En `Judges xiii. 18.`, while La `Judæ v. 20.` sits against En `Jude 20.` The parallel text is what settles these, and it is the reason both layers must be parsed rather than one keyed off the other.",
 "_layers_differ": "The two volumes do NOT share an abbreviation table and must be parsed with separate maps. Sumner also varies WITHIN a layer: Levit./Lev., Numb./Num., Neh./Nehem., Micah/Mic., Esther/Esth., Lam./Lament., Hos./Hosea, 1 John/1 John.",
 "_watch_Reg": "✅ CONFIRMED AT PLATE 2026-08-28. The Latin uses `1 Reg.`/`2 Reg.` for 1-2 KINGS while separately using `1 Sam.`/`2 Sam.` for Samuel — NOT the Vulgate convention (Vulg. 1-4 Regum = 1-2 Samuel + 1-2 Kings). Read off La 15 and La 17 against En 22 and En 25 (I.ii, freshly rendered): La `1 Reg. viii. 27. cœli cœlorum non capiunt te` sits against En `1 Kings viii. 27. the heaven and heaven of heavens cannot contain thee` — 1 Kings 8:27, and 1 Samuel 8 has only 22 verses, so the Samuel reading is impossible on the digits alone. La 17 confirms the pair together in one paragraph: `1 Reg. viii. 60.` / `2 Reg. xix. 15.` against En 25's `1 Kings viii. 60.` / `2 Kings xix. 15.`, both quoting Solomon's dedication and Hezekiah's prayer. M4-RUNBOOK §7 step 1 is discharged.",
 "_not_a_book": {
  "Cap.": "⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-28 — this entry was WRONG. `cap.`/`Cap.` is not a book and is not normally Milton citing his own treatise: it is a CHAPTER CONTINUATION carrying the current book forward. `Exod. xvii. 16 ... Cap. iii. 14.` is Exodus 3:14, `Deut. iv. 35 ... cap. vi. 4.` is Deuteronomy 6:4, `Gen. xii. 13 ... cap. xiv. 22, 23.` is Genesis 14. Dropping it as a non-book would have silently lost a whole continuation class. The self-reference use is real but rare and is always governed — `Vide supra lib. 1. cap. xxvii.`, `ut superiore libro cap. x.` — four instances in 21 chunks, all in Book II; build-citations.py excludes exactly those.",
  "Sic": "`Sic in MS.` — the opening of Sumner's Latin textual notes (CONVENTIONS §5a).",
  "Christ.": "a word of the text, not a citation."
 },
 "_numeral_dropped": {
  "note": "Tokens seen WITHOUT their leading numeral. Almost certainly continuations where the numeral is carried from the preceding citation, but UNRESOLVED — do not guess a number. Per the Bonaventure rule, an unresolvable citation becomes a QA line, never a record with an invented target.",
